Syntax v0.4 Continuation Plan
===============================================================================

Status: proposed implementation plan.

Planning baseline: `feature/syntax` at `4560e5e6c`, rebased onto
`integration/v0.4` after the R1-R6 stack. Pull request 812 is green at this
baseline. This document changes the delivery plan only; it does not change a
parser entry point, grammar rule, syntax registry, or runtime behavior.

This plan supersedes the old assumption that the remaining recursive-core work
could be delivered as one Phase 2I-D change followed directly by Phase 2I-E
activation. Recovery, typed syntax, compatibility lowering, certification,
registry activation, document-root completion, production cutover, and legacy
retirement now have independent gates.

1. Current state
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The canonical inventory still contains exactly 539 rules:

```text
syntax parity-verified     323
syntax ported                5
unported                   211
                           ---
total                      539
```

The completed canonical phases therefore cover 328 / 539 rules. The generated
Phase 2I recursive component contains 80 of the 211 unported rules. Phase 2I-C
recognizes clean input for all 80 rules with the canonical event parser, but it
intentionally activates none of them. Those rows remain `unported` with
`pending` lowering and no phase assignment.

The recursive component still lacks local malformed-input recovery, typed
red-tree views, compatibility lowering, direct differential certification, and
atomic registry activation.

The only supported public canonical root is `Canonical + Grammar`. The
lossless document root is still `Prototype + Document`. The shipping
`mech_syntax::parser::parse(&str) -> MResult<mech_core::Program>` entry point
still uses the legacy Nom parser.

That shipping entry point is a shared source boundary. Current callers include:

- CLI input classification and source/HTML formatting;
- file and in-memory resolution, import discovery, and `SourceIndex` creation;
- interactive source accumulation and runtime program compilation;
- engine planning and source-based compiler tests; and
- formatter round trips and Mechdown rendering.

R1-R6 changed the semantic pipeline downstream of `mech_core::Program`.
Canonical program artifacts, Type System v1, compiler IR, memory plans, runtime
values, and execution ownership are now explicit later-stage contracts. The
syntax migration must preserve that separation.

2. Decisions fixed by this plan
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The compatibility target for canonical syntax is the existing
`mech_core::Program` syntax AST and its child node types. Syntax code does not
construct `SourceProgram`, `ExpressionIR`, `Value`, bytecode, memory plans, or
runtime cells. Those remain owned by compiler and runtime stages that already
consume `Program`.

The canonical grammar in `docs/design/specification.mec` remains normative.
The legacy parser is the executable behavior oracle for compatibility until the
production cutover. A deliberate language correction must be recorded in the
existing discrepancy/conformance ledger and tested as an explicit expected
difference. `MATCH-001` remains the only accepted Phase 2I-C correction.

Phase 2I stays one atomic strongly connected component. No subset of its 80
rules is registered as ported. Atomic registration is an inventory claim; it is
not permission to switch the public parser.

The shipping `parse` signature and `Program` result remain stable through the
cutover. Canonical lossless snapshots, diagnostics, and typed syntax may gain
separate APIs, but existing consumers do not migrate in the same change.

The production parser switches only after a complete canonical document root
can parse, strictly validate, and lower a whole source into `Program`. Direct
fragment roots remain test and tool surfaces and cannot silently become the
shipping language boundary.

3. Non-goals
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This syntax continuation does not:

- change bytecode-v1, canonical encoding, dynamic ABI, native linkage, program
  artifact formats, Type System v1, or memory planner/runtime contracts;
- create runtime `Value` instances or compiler-owned cells during parsing or
  compatibility lowering;
- restore removed interpreter or REPL parser paths;
- make a partial Phase 2I registry claim;
- treat clean recognition as sufficient evidence for shipping activation;
- change accepted syntax without an explicit grammar and correction-ledger
  update; or
- remove the legacy parser while a production consumer still depends on it.

4. Delivery sequence
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

S0. Rebaseline and contract record
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Record this plan and update the older Phase 2I status documents to point to it.
Freeze the green rebased baseline and current inventory counts. This step
contains no parser behavior.

Exit gate:

- the plan, architecture overview, recursive-core contract, and grammar-audit
  status agree about the new sequence;
- generated inventories remain unchanged; and
- the branch remains green against `integration/v0.4`.

S1. Source-boundary and compatibility census
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Turn the current caller survey into an enforced contract before adding parser
behavior.

Deliverables:

- inventory every production call to `mech_syntax::parser::parse`, including
  input trimming, feature gates, empty input, and error expectations;
- map each of the 80 Phase 2I rules to its exact legacy output type, transparent
  behavior, or conditional node policy;
- define one syntax-owned strict-lowering failure schema for diagnostics,
  erroneous trees, missing children, invalid token text, and range failures;
- add a differential test adapter that can invoke legacy direct rules only as a
  test oracle; and
- freeze representative whole-source fixtures for CLI classification,
  formatter rendering, source indexing, resolver dependencies, interactive
  accumulation, and compiler planning.

The adapter is never a canonical production dependency. Nom types remain
outside canonical parser, AST, and lowerer modules.

Exit gate:

- every shipping caller has a named fixture or an explicit reason it is covered
  by another caller contract;
- every Phase 2I rule has a lowering disposition; and
- no canonical production module imports or invokes legacy parser code.

S2. Recursive-core recovery
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Add local recovery for the complete 80-rule component using the ownership rules
already frozen in `recursive-executable-core.mec`.

Deliverables:

- `MISSING` insertion only at legitimate committed boundaries;
- unexpected source retained beneath `ERROR` nodes;
- delimiter-aware abandonment to the owning ancestor restart root;
- stable rule/context diagnostics and recovery actions;
- progress enforcement for every parse and recovery loop; and
- hard fuel, nesting, event, diagnostic, and recovery-byte limits.

Clean trees must remain byte-for-byte and structurally stable. Recovery must
not change complete-form selection for tuples, parentheticals, matrices,
comprehensions, records, maps, sets, calls, slices, atoms, or tuple structures.

Exit gate:

- every one of the 80 direct roots has malformed-input coverage;
- clean snapshots remain unchanged from Phase 2I-C;
- recovered snapshots reproduce every source byte exactly once;
- committed failures retain their diagnostic and partial parent structure; and
- adversarial shared-prefix and nesting cases stay within documented bounds.

All 80 registry rows remain inactive.

S3. Typed recursive syntax
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Add zero-copy typed red-tree views under
`document/ast/recursive_core/`, mirroring the frozen parser ownership.

Deliverables:

- typed casts for all unconditional and conditional Phase 2I node kinds;
- optional accessors for required children that may be absent in live syntax;
- ordered repeated-child accessors where grammar order carries meaning;
- token and delimiter access where tools need source-preserving edits; and
- explicit handling of the two transparent aliases without invented wrappers.

Exit gate:

- schema-driven coverage proves that every node-valued row has a typed view;
- accessors cover clean, missing, error, and trivia-bearing forms;
- casts reject unrelated kinds; and
- typed access does not allocate a second tree or copy source text.

All 80 registry rows remain inactive.

S4. Strict compatibility lowering
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Add compatibility lowering under
`document/lower/legacy/recursive_core/`, mirroring parser ownership.

Deliverables:

- lower every clean Phase 2I direct value to the existing `mech_core::nodes`
  output recorded by the S1 map;
- preserve token kinds, identifiers, literal values, ordering, source ranges,
  and legacy alternative selection;
- reject diagnostics, `ERROR`, `MISSING`, incomplete required children, and
  invalid token text through the common lowering diagnostic schema;
- keep feature-dependent outputs behind the shipping parser's feature
  boundaries; and
- expose package-local direct-rule lowering only for certification until a
  canonical whole-document root exists.

The lowerer ends at syntax AST values. Compiler IR and runtime values remain
downstream.

Exit gate:

- all 80 rows have executable lowering coverage or a schema-backed transparent
  disposition;
- recovered trees fail without panicking or manufacturing semantic values;
- lowered ranges match legacy results; and
- full, base, Mika-only, and reduced/no-std checks preserve their boundaries.

All 80 registry rows remain inactive.

S5. Differential certification
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Certify the component before changing inventory status.

Deliverables:

- direct accepted-input parity between legacy results and canonical
  parse-plus-lower results for every Phase 2I rule;
- accepted/rejected classification parity across the grammar corpus;
- structural, value, ordering, and source-range comparisons;
- ambiguity cases for every shared prefix and complete-form selection rule;
- property and generated cases for nesting, repeated operands, trivia, Unicode,
  delimiters, and boundary whitespace;
- one expected-difference assertion per correction-ledger entry; and
- downstream canaries for the nearest applicable consumer of each lowered
  output family.

Downstream canaries compose lowered values through existing syntax AST parents
where that parent is available. A rule blocked by an unported parent remains a
direct certification case; no temporary shipping root is added.

Exit gate:

- the 80-rule matrix passes under full and base feature profiles;
- no unclassified legacy/canonical difference remains;
- ambiguity and resource-limit suites pass; and
- S1 whole-source fixtures remain unchanged under the shipping legacy path.

All 80 registry rows remain inactive until this gate is complete.

S6. Atomic Phase 2I activation
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Update `ports.tsv` for all 80 rules in one change and regenerate the canonical
port registry. Each member becomes Phase 2I with parity-verified syntax and
lowering.

Expected inventory after activation:

```text
syntax parity-verified     403
syntax ported                5
unported                   131
                           ---
total                      539

canonical syntax progress  408 / 539
```

Exit gate:

- the generated SCC report still identifies exactly the reviewed 80 members;
- every row has parse, recovery, typed-view, lowering, direct parity, ambiguity,
  and resource-limit evidence;
- generated registries and inventory tests pass; and
- the public root matrix is unchanged.

This milestone does not switch `mech_syntax::parser::parse`.

S7. Complete the canonical document-root closure
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Regenerate dependency and SCC reports after Phase 2I activation. The 131 rows
that remain unported are an inventory count, not an assumption that every row
belongs to the shipping document-root closure. Partition them into generated
document dependencies, other maintained roots, and historical or non-syntax
entries. Give every row an explicit current disposition. Port the document
closure in generated dependency order, one complete SCC or closed acyclic batch
at a time. Handwritten family totals do not override the graph.

Every batch repeats the same gates at its own scale:

```text
schema -> clean parse -> recovery -> typed views -> strict lowering
       -> differential certification -> atomic batch activation
```

The objective is a complete canonical `Document` root that handles sibling
Mech and Mechdown constructs and strictly lowers whole valid sources to
`mech_core::Program`. It preserves title, section, annotation, fenced-code,
import/export/context, comment, and source-range behavior required by callers.

Exit gate:

- `Canonical + Document` is a supported internal root;
- every rule needed by that root is registered and certified;
- every remaining inventory row has a reviewed port, maintained-root,
  not-applicable, or historical disposition;
- whole-source lowering produces `Program` values equivalent to the shipping
  parser across the repository corpus and S1 fixtures;
- malformed documents produce total lossless snapshots and diagnostics; and
- no shipping caller has changed routes yet.

S8. Shadow production route and cutover
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Add one syntax-owned compatibility route:

```text
source text
  -> canonical document snapshot
  -> strict validation
  -> compatibility lowering
  -> mech_core::Program
```

Run it beside the legacy route in tests and nonshipping validation. Compare:

- parse success and failure classification;
- `Program` structure, values, ordering, and source ranges;
- diagnostic location and caller-visible error behavior;
- formatter source and HTML output;
- CLI inline-source classification;
- `SourceIndex`, imports, exports, contexts, scopes, and dependencies;
- interactive append/reparse behavior; and
- compiler planning and canonical artifact output.

After the shadow matrix has no unclassified differences, change the existing
`mech_syntax::parser::parse` implementation to the canonical route while
retaining its public signature. Make the route change in one reversible commit.

Exit gate:

- the full corpus and all S1 consumer fixtures pass through the canonical route;
- static distribution, native, WASM source, no-std, and warning jobs are green;
- compiler artifact and runtime canaries show no semantic contract drift; and
- production code has one selected source-to-`Program` route.

S9. Legacy parser retirement
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Retire legacy parsing only after the canonical route has shipped green and no
production caller selects the old implementation.

Deliverables:

- remove legacy direct-rule adapters after their replacement corpus is
  self-sufficient;
- remove Nom parser modules and dependencies in dependency-safe batches;
- remove obsolete parser feature coupling and dead compatibility helpers;
- update formatter and source-index tests to name the canonical route; and
- preserve the Phase 0 audit as historical provenance.

Exit gate:

- repository-wide scans find no production Nom parser path;
- all 539 canonical rules have a current disposition;
- all shipping source consumers use the canonical route; and
- full CI and distribution validation are green without legacy parser code.

5. Validation policy
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Each milestone runs the checks that prove its local gate:

- generated rule, port, dependency, and SCC inventories;
- full and base syntax test profiles;
- grammar conformance under the explicit compiler/program feature set;
- Mika-only and reduced/no-std boundary checks;
- formatting, static architecture, module layout, and warning policy; and
- focused complexity, ambiguity, recovery, typed-view, lowering, or parity
  suites added by that milestone.

S6, S8, and S9 additionally require the full integration workflow because they
change an authoritative registry, shipping route, or dependencies. S8 also
requires static native and WASM source distributions plus the end-to-end
compiler/runtime canaries used by `integration/v0.4`.

Tests compare stable structure, values, ranges, diagnostic codes, and recovery
actions. English diagnostic wording is not a compatibility oracle.

6. Review and branch shape
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Use one reviewable branch per numbered milestone. Recovery, typed syntax, and
lowering stay separate even if later work begins in a stack. A branch targets
the last accepted syntax milestone and is rebased when its base moves.

Generated files and source inventories change together. Phase 2I activation is
isolated from implementation so reviewers can verify the exact 80-row status
transition. The production cutover is isolated from parser completion so it can
be reviewed and reverted without discarding canonical syntax work.

7. Completion definition
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The syntax continuation is complete when the canonical document parser is the
only production source-to-`Program` route, all 539 canonical rules have a
current reviewed disposition, every shipping consumer passes through the
canonical compatibility boundary, and legacy Nom parsing is absent from
production dependencies.
